==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|accomplisht}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
* First attested in the late 15th century, from {{suffix|en|accomplish|ed}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkɑm.plɪʃt/|a=GenAm}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkʌm.plɪʃt/|/əˈkɒm.plɪʃt/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-accomplished.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Completed; [[effected]]; [[established]].
#: ''an '''accomplished''' fact''
# Having many [[accomplishment]]s, often as a result of study or training.
#: ''an '''accomplished''' scholar'', ''an '''accomplished''' villain''
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=IV|line=660|passage=Daughter of God and Man, '''accompliſht''' ''Eve'',|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Pioneers and Makers of Arkansas|author=Josiah Hazen Shinn|year=1967|page=335|ISBN=0806303115|passage=When Margaret Frances Desha returned to Batesville, the most '''accomplished''' woman of the town, she was wooed and won by William French Denton, a distinguished lawyer of Batesville, and a gift of Tennessee to Arkansas growth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A History of Young People in the West - Volume 1|author=Giovanni Levi; Jean-Claude Schmitt|year=1997|page=36|ISBN=0674404076|passage=The presence of the dog and hart further clarifies the meaning of these scenes: hunting was one of the ways in which an '''accomplished''' young man could assert himself.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Young Lady's Guide to Charm, Style & Femininity|author=Keisha Clark|year=2007|page=82|ISBN=1602472904|passage=Knowing this, makes all the difference in the world as to how you will be received as an '''accomplished''' young lady when you are presented to the world, and later presented to your Adam.}}
# Showing skill and artistry.
#: ''an '''accomplished''' first novel''
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|III|pages=7–8|pageref=7|passage="Oh! yes—the handsomest young lady that ever was seen; and so '''accomplished'''!—She plays and sings all day long. In the next room is a new instrument just come down for her—a present from my master; she comes here to-morrow with him."}}

